          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.741,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Quadrilaterals"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.09,
            0.529,
            0.114
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.1 Rectangles and Squares"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.123,
            0.64,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": "We know what rectangles are. Let us define them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.149,
            0.665,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": "**Rectangle:** A rectangle is a quadrilateral in which—"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.173,
            0.648,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "(i) The angles are all right angles (90°), and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.197,
            0.63,
            0.218
          ],
          "content": "(ii) The opposite sides are of equal length."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.22,
            0.848,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "The definition precisely states the conditions a quadrilateral has to satisfy to be called a rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.269,
            0.583,
            0.29
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Are there other ways to define a rectangle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.294,
            0.851,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider the following problem related to the construction of rectangles."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.348,
            0.432,
            0.369
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A Carpenter’s Problem"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.375,
            0.501,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "？ A carpenter needs to put together two thin strips of wood, as shown in Fig. 1, so that when a thread is passed through their endpoints, it forms a rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.473,
            0.502,
            0.547
          ],
          "content": "She already has one 8 cm long strip. What should be the length of the other strip? Where should they both be joined?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.508,
            0.373,
            0.897,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.548,
            0.502,
            0.584
          ],
          "content": "Let us first model the structure that the carpenter has to make. The"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.583,
            0.849,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "strips can be modelled as line segments. They are the diagonals of the quadrilateral formed by their endpoints. For the quadrilateral to be a rectangle, we need to answer the following questions —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.642,
            0.576,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "1. What is the length of the other diagonal?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.671,
            0.702,
            0.693
          ],
          "content": "2. What is the point of intersection of the two diagonals?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.7,
            0.659,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "3. What should the angle be between the diagonals?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.729,
            0.503,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "一 Let us answer these questions using geometric reasoning (deduction). If that is challenging, try to construct/ measure some rectangles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.808,
            0.503,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "To find the answers to these questions, let us suppose that we have placed the diagonals such that their endpoints form the vertices of a rectangle, as shown in Fig. 2."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.506,
            0.735,
            0.915,
            0.914
          ],
